{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787938678969,"sessionID":"ses_fb68f715dffenWbrsP50CKBzBA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_d79aef7b880946d8bcef1025","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/wood_listener-architecture/tasks.md","limit":20},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/wood_listener-architecture/tasks.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Tasks: listener architecture\n2: \n3: id: wood\n4: status: open\n5: analyst: wood\n6: date: 2026-08-27\n7: \n8: <!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n9: \n10: ## Task breakdown\n11: \n12: <!-- TASK-1: Project registry in a new daemon package. Add a new internal package for the daemon (suggested name src/internal/serve — the home for the registry, HTTP server, handlers, auth, audit and locks; one package for all of it, mirroring how internal/session owns the launcher). Registry type: loads an explicit config file of project roots at startup — NO scanning, NO auto-adopting directories the daemon finds (brief scope item 2). Suggested config location and shape: <manigot checkout>/config/serve.json (config.Dir()) holding {\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\", ...]}, path overridable via --registry (TASK-2's flag) — the brief leaves the location open, confirm the choice while implementing. Each entry validated as an existing directory (a registered project needs docs/ for jobs, but only warn on that — a root without docs/ lists no jobs rather than failing startup). Registrations are read once at startup; changing the file means editing and restarting (v1). Provide Projects() (ordered copy) and a Project(name) lookup so every handler resolves ONLY against registered roots — the single choke point TASK-7's zero-path-inputs enforcement builds on. Include registry_test.go: parses the config, validates roots, rejects a missing file (empty registry, not an error), rejects non-directory entries, lookup by exact path and by base name.\n13:      files: src/internal/serve/registry.go (new), src/internal/serve/registry_test.go (new)\n14:      depends: none\n15:      risk: low — pure config read + validation, no system interaction beyond os.Stat\n16: \n17: TASK-2: `mg serve` subcommand scaffold. Add `serve` to the dispatcher switch in src/cmd/mg/main.go (next to tui/jdi — a long-running in-process command) and to printHelp's command list; new src/cmd/mg/serve.go implementing runServe: flag parsing (--addr/--port with a sensible localhost default such as 127.0.0.1:8080, --registry <path> overriding the config location, --token <value> — token read precedence: flag, then MG_SERVE_TOKEN via config.EnvValue, see TASK-6), builds the Registry (TASK-1), constructs the Server (TASK-3) passing main's `version` var (internal/serve cannot import package main — the health endpoint needs it), enforces the bind/auth startup guard (TASK-6), then blocks on the serve loop and shuts down gracefully on SIGINT/SIGTERM (http.Server.Shutdown with a bounded drain). Long-running-process lifecycle is new territory for the single binary — the signal-handling and shutdown path must be testable (an httptest-able Serve/Shutdown seam, not a bare http.ListenAndServe call in main).\n18:      files: src/cmd/mg/main.go, src/cmd/mg/serve.go (new), src/cmd/mg/serve_test.go (new)\n19:      depends: TASK-1, TASK-3, TASK-6 (wires them; can be stubbed against their signatures and landed last among the code tasks)\n20:      risk: medium — blocking serve + graceful shutdown is a new process shape for mg; the shutdown drain must be bounded so a hung request cannot wedge the exit\n\n(Showing lines 1-20 of 67. Use offset=21 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Tasks: listener architecture\n\nid: wood\nstatus: open\nanalyst: wood\ndate: 2026-08-27\n\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n\n## Task breakdown\n\n<!-- TASK-1: Project registry in a new daemon package. Add a new internal package for the daemon (suggested name src/internal/serve — the home for 