{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787938747423,"sessionID":"ses_fb68c3d29ffeIO0HMfdSuVup5i","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_c9be727e8cdd405c91937423","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md","offset":505,"limit":60},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n505: either way.\n506: \n507: ### Listener / control plane\n508: \n509: `mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon that exposes a **read-only**\n510: control API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\n511: native GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\n512: from a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence (`mg serve` + project\n513: registry + read-only API + localhost default + audit log + serialization\n514: skeleton); mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\n515: any frontend are later jobs. The daemon is additive — the TUI stays\n516: in-process and every existing command keeps working untouched — and it is a\n517: **new trust boundary** with root-adjacent power (it drives docker and git and\n518: holds subscription credentials), so the v1 surface is deliberately read-only\n519: and the security invariants below are non-negotiable.\n520: \n521: - **Project registry.** An explicit config file, `<checkout>/config/serve.json`\n522:   (overridable via `--registry`), holding `{\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\", ...]}`.\n523:   No scanning, no auto-adopting directories the daemon finds. Registrations\n524:   are read once at startup; changing them means editing the file and\n525:   restarting (v1). Each entry is validated as an existing directory at\n526:   startup; a root without `docs/` is accepted (it lists no jobs rather than\n527:   failing startup). Every handler resolves ONLY against the registered roots —\n528:   the single choke point the zero-path-inputs rule builds on.\n529: - **Read-only API.** The state the TUI renders, over HTTP as JSON + raw\n530:   markdown (never ANSI): `GET /health` (version, docker image present,\n531:   per-profile readiness), `GET /projects`, `GET /projects/<p>/jobs` (the\n532:   TUI's info design — id/status/stage/type/date/title — plus per-job mg-jdi\n533:   state), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/files/<brief|tasks|implementation|verdict>`,\n534:   `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/jdi` (status + `run.log` + `session.log` tails),\n535:   `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/diff` (the `mg diff` quick eyeball, `?full=1`\n536:   for the patch), and `GET /projects/<p>/agents` (name + description only).\n537: - **Binding + auth.** Binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default, tokenless — the\n538:   machine's own user is the trust boundary, as it is for the CLI. A\n539:   non-loopback bind (neither 127.0.0.0/8 nor `::1`) REQUIRES a bearer token\n540:   (`--token`, else `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN` via the config/env layer) or the daemon\n541:   refuses to start — the guard is unskippable, there is no flag that turns it\n542:   off. When configured, every request must carry `Authorization: Bearer\n543:   <token>`, compared in constant time. TLS is the reverse proxy's job\n544:   (Caddy/nginx) — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates\n545:   TLS, the only safe posture behind a proxy. Tokens are configured\n546:   out-of-band; the API never issues, creates, or returns one.\n547: - **Audit trail.** Every request is logged — timestamp, client IP, method +\n548:   path, auth outcome (authed / tokenless / 401), response status — to the\n549:   daemon's stderr by default. The Authorization header, the token, and request\n550:   bodies are never logged.\n551: - **Security invariants.** (1) **Zero path inputs** — URL segments are\n552:   validated as plain identifiers and matched against the registry / discovered\n553:   jobs / the file whitelist; they are never joined into filesystem paths, and\n554:   traversal in any encoding is rejected. (2) **Credentials never returned** —\n555:   no `.env` content, no keys, no tokens in any response, ever (enforced by\n556:   tests over the whole surface). (3) **Per-project serialization** —\n557:   `serve.ProjectLocks` (keyed by registered root) is the concurrency pattern\n558:   job two's mutating handlers MUST use; `internal/git` has no locking today,\n559:   and v1 read endpoints deliberately take no locks.\n560: \n561: ### Config files\n562: \n563: - `manigot/.env` (gitignored) — credentials and defaults for the profiles\n564:   (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`/`CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_UUID`/`CLAUDE_EMAIL`/\n\n(Showing lines 505-564 of 759. Use offset=565 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"either way.\n\n### Listener / control plane\n\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon that exposes a **read-only**\ncontrol API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\nfrom a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence (`mg serve` + project\nregistry + read-only API + localhost default + audit log + serialization\nskeleton); mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\nany frontend are later jobs. The daemon is additive — the TUI stays\nin-process and every existing command keeps working untouched — and it is a\n**new trust boundary** with root-adjacent power (it drives docker and git and\nholds subscription credentials), so the v1 surface is deliberately read-only\nand the security invariants below are non-negotiable.\n\n- **Project registry.** An explicit config file, `<checkout>/config/serve.json`\n  (overridable via `--registry`), holding `{\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\", ...]}`.\n  No scanning, no auto-adopting directories the daemon finds. Registrations\n  are read once at startup; changing them means editing the file and","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md","text":"either way.\n\n### Listener / control plane\n\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon that exposes a **read-only**\ncontrol API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\nfrom a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence (`mg serve` + project\nregistry + read-only API + localhost default + audit log + serialization\nskeleton); mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\nany frontend are later jobs. The daemon is additive — the TUI stays\nin-process and every existing command keeps working untouched — and it is a\n**new trust boundary** with root-adjacent power (it drives docker and git and\nholds subscription credentials), so the v1 surface is deliberately read-only\nand the security invariants below are non-negotiable.\n\n- **Project registry.** An explicit config file, `<checkout>/config/serve.json`\n  (overridable via `--registry`), holding `{\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\", ...]}`.\n  No scanning, no auto-adopting directories the daemon finds. Registrations\n  are read once at startup; changing them means editing the file and\n  restarting (v1). Each entry is validated as an existing directory at\n  startup; a root without `docs/` is accepted (it lists no jobs rather than\n  failing startup). Every handler resolves ONLY against the registered roots —\n  the single choke point the zero-path-inputs rule builds on.\n- **Read-only API.** The state the TUI renders, over HTTP as JSON + raw\n  markdown (never ANSI): `GET /health` (version, docker image present,\n  per-profile readiness), `GET /projects`, `GET /projects/<p>/jobs` (the\n  TUI's info design — id/status/stage/type/date/title — plus per-job mg-jdi\n  state), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/files/<brief|tasks|implementation|verdict>`,\n  `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/jdi` (status + `run.log` + `session.log` tails),\n  `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/diff` (the `mg diff` quick eyeball, `?full=1`\n  for the patch), and `GET /projects/<p>/agents` (name + description only).\n- **Binding + auth.** Binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default, tokenless — the\n  machine's own user is the trust boundary, as it is for the CLI. A\n  non-loopback bind (neither 127.0.0.0/8 nor `::1`) REQUIRES a bearer token\n  (`--token`, else `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN` via the config/env layer) or the daemon\n  refuses to start — the guard is unskippable, there is no flag that turns it\n  off. When configured, every request must carry `Authorization: Bearer\n  <token>`, compared in constant time. TLS is the reverse proxy's job\n  (Caddy/nginx) — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates\n  TLS, the only safe posture behind a proxy. Tokens are configured\n  out-of-band; the API never issues, creates, or returns one.\n- **Audit trail.** Every request is logged — timestamp, client IP, method +\n  path, auth outcome (authed / tokenless / 401), response status — to the\n  daemon's stderr by default. The Authorization header, the token, and request\n  bodies are never logged.\n- **Security invariants.** (1) **Zero path inputs** — URL segments are\n  validated as plain identifiers and matched against the registry / discovered\n  jobs / the file whitelist; they are never joined into filesystem paths, and\n  traversal in any encoding is rejected. (2) **Credentials never returned** —\n  no `.env` content, no keys, no tokens in any response, ever (enforced by\n  tests over the whole surface). (3) **Per-project serialization** —\n  `serve.ProjectLocks` (keyed by registered root) is the concurrency pattern\n  job two's mutating handlers MUST use; `internal/git` has no locking today,\n  and v1 read endpoints deliberately take no locks.\n\n### Config files\n\n- `manigot/.env` (gitignored) — credentials and defaults for the profiles\n  (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`/`CLAUDE_ACCOUNT_UUID`/`CLAUDE_EMAIL`/","lineStart":505,"lineEnd":564,"totalLines":759,"truncated":true}},"title":"docs/AGENTS.md","time":{"start":1787938747418,"end":1787938747422}},"id":"prt_0497431c2001hTALGdWEAFeVRe","sessionID":"ses_fb68c3d29ffeIO0HMfdSuVup5i","messageID":"msg_049742df2001BvWe0RrHbDhT0N"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787938747635,"sessionID":"ses_fb68c3d29ffeIO0HMfdSuVup5i","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_3c97c892cc9847b895ea4d93","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/README.md","offset":1145,"limit":60},"output":"<path>/workspace/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1145: `j`-launched run has no terminal to ring into, so the TUI rings it instead,\n1146: on its own next poll, the first time it notices that job's status turn into\n1147: `finished` or `needs human` — once per fresh stop, not on every poll while\n1148: it stays stopped, and never for a job that was already stopped before this\n1149: TUI session started watching it.\n1150: \n1151: ---\n1152: \n1153: ## Listener\n1154: \n1155: `mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a **read-only**\n1156: control API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\n1157: native GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\n1158: from a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence; the API is read-only\n1159: by design (mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\n1160: any frontend are later jobs — see the out-of-scope list in\n1161: [`docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md`](docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md)).\n1162: \n1163: **Registry.** An explicit config file of project roots — no scanning, no\n1164: auto-adopting directories the daemon finds:\n1165: \n1166: ```bash\n1167: # <manigot checkout>/config/serve.json  (override with --registry)\n1168: {\"projects\": [\"/abs/project/root\", ...]}\n1169: ```\n1170: \n1171: Registrations are read once at startup; changing them means editing the file\n1172: and restarting (v1). Each entry must be an existing directory; a root without\n1173: `docs/` is accepted and simply lists no jobs.\n1174: \n1175: **Binding + auth.** The daemon binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default and is\n1176: tokenless in that shape — the machine's own user is the trust boundary, as it\n1177: is for the CLI. A non-loopback bind (a VPS) **requires** a bearer token —\n1178: `--token`, else `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN` — or the daemon refuses to start; there is\n1179: no flag that turns that guard off. When a token is configured, every request\n1180: must carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>` (compared in constant time); a\n1181: missing or wrong token is a `401`. TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy /\n1182: nginx) — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates TLS, the\n1183: only safe posture behind a proxy. A token without TLS is no protection.\n1184: \n1185: **API.** `GET /health` (version, docker image present, per-profile\n1186: readiness), `GET /projects`, `GET /projects/<p>/jobs` (id / status / stage /\n1187: type / date / title, plus per-job mg-jdi state),\n1188: `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/files/<brief|tasks|implementation|verdict>`\n1189: (raw markdown), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/jdi` (status + `run.log` +\n1190: `session.log` tails), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/diff` (the `mg diff` quick\n1191: eyeball, `?full=1` for the patch), and `GET /projects/<p>/agents`.\n1192: \n1193: **Security invariants** (non-negotiable): URL segments are validated as\n1194: plain identifiers and resolved only against the registry / discovered jobs /\n1195: the file whitelist — never joined into filesystem paths, and traversal in any\n1196: encoding is rejected; credentials are never returned in any response and never\n1197: logged (each request is audit-logged with timestamp, client, operation, auth\n1198: outcome and status — never the token); and mutating operations (a later job)\n1199: must serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks` pattern.\n1200: \n1201: ---\n1202: \n1203: ## Rebuilding\n1204: \n\n(Showing lines 1145-1204 of 1222. Use offset=1205 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"`j`-launched run has no terminal to ring into, so the TUI rings it instead,\non its own next poll, the first time it notices that job's status turn into\n`finished` or `needs human` — once per fresh stop, not on every poll while\nit stays stopped, and never for a job that was already stopped before this\nTUI session started watching it.\n\n---\n\n## Listener\n\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a **read-only**\ncontrol API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\nfrom a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence; the API is read-only\nby design (mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\nany frontend are later jobs — see the out-of-scope list in\n[`docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md`](docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md)).\n\n**Registry.** An explicit config file of project roots — no scanning, no\nauto-adopting directories the daemon finds:","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/README.md","text":"`j`-launched run has no terminal to ring into, so the TUI rings it instead,\non its own next poll, the first time it notices that job's status turn into\n`finished` or `needs human` — once per fresh stop, not on every poll while\nit stays stopped, and never for a job that was already stopped before this\nTUI session started watching it.\n\n---\n\n## Listener\n\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a **read-only**\ncontrol API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\nfrom a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence; the API is read-only\nby design (mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\nany frontend are later jobs — see the out-of-scope list in\n[`docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md`](docs/jobs/wood_listener-architecture/brief.md)).\n\n**Registry.** An explicit config file of project roots — no scanning, no\nauto-adopting directories the daemon finds:\n\n```bash\n# <manigot checkout>/config/serve.json  (override with --registry)\n{\"projects\": [\"/abs/project/root\", ...]}\n```\n\nRegistrations are read once at startup; changing them means editing the file\nand restarting (v1). Each entry must be an existing directory; a root without\n`docs/` is accepted and simply lists no jobs.\n\n**Binding + auth.** The daemon binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default and is\ntokenless in that shape — the machine's own user is the trust boundary, as it\nis for the CLI. A non-loopback bind (a VPS) **requires** a bearer token —\n`--token`, else `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN` — or the daemon refuses to start; there is\nno flag that turns that guard off. When a token is configured, every request\nmust carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>` (compared in constant time); a\nmissing or wrong token is a `401`. TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy /\nnginx) — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates TLS, the\nonly safe posture behind a proxy. A token without TLS is no protection.\n\n**API.** `GET /health` (version, docker image present, per-profile\nreadiness), `GET /projects`, `GET /projects/<p>/jobs` (id / status / stage /\ntype / date / title, plus per-job mg-jdi state),\n`GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/files/<brief|tasks|implementation|verdict>`\n(raw markdown), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/jdi` (status + `run.log` +\n`session.log` tails), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/diff` (the `mg diff` quick\neyeball, `?full=1` for the patch), and `GET /projects/<p>/agents`.\n\n**Security invariants** (non-negotiable): URL segments are validated as\nplain identifiers and resolved only against the registry / discovered jobs /\nthe file whitelist — never joined into filesystem paths, and traversal in any\nencoding is rejected; credentials are never returned in any response and never\nlogged (each request is audit-logged with timestamp, client, operation, auth\noutcome and status — never the token); and mutating operations (a later job)\nmust serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks` pattern.\n\n---\n\n## Rebuilding\n","lineStart":1145,"lineEnd":1204,"totalLines":1222,"truncated":true}},"title":"README.md","time":{"start":1787938747626,"end":1787938747634}},"id":"prt_04974341b001xR5cnQVuYJS7GE","sessionID":"ses_fb68c3d29ffeIO0HMfdSuVup5i","messageID":"msg_049742df2001BvWe0RrHbDhT0N"}}
