{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vb2zMU7jATUbG9coCTsvVB","type":"tool_result","content":"510\tcontrol API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\n511\tnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\n512\tfrom a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence (`mg serve` + project\n513\tregistry + read-only API + localhost default + audit log + serialization\n514\tskeleton); mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\n515\tany frontend are later jobs. The daemon is additive — the TUI stays\n516\tin-process and every existing command keeps working untouched — and it is a\n517\t**new trust boundary** with root-adjacent power (it drives docker and git and\n518\tholds subscription credentials), so the v1 surface is deliberately read-only\n519\tand the security invariants below are non-negotiable.\n520\t\n521\t- **Project registry.** An explicit config file, `<checkout>/config/serve.json`\n522\t  (overridable via `--registry`), holding `{\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\", ...]}`.\n523\t  No scanning, no auto-adopting directories the daemon finds. Registrations\n524\t  are read once at startup; changing them means editing the file and\n525\t  restarting (v1). Each entry is validated as an existing directory at\n526\t  startup; a root without `docs/` is accepted (it lists no jobs rather than\n527\t  failing startup). Every handler resolves ONLY against the registered roots —\n528\t  the single choke point the zero-path-inputs rule builds on.\n529\t- **Read-only API.** The state the TUI renders, over HTTP as JSON + raw\n530\t  markdown (never ANSI): `GET /health` (version, docker image present,\n531\t  per-profile readiness), `GET /projects`, `GET /projects/<p>/jobs` (the\n532\t  TUI's info design — id/status/stage/type/date/title — plus per-job mg-jdi\n533\t  state), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/files/<brief|tasks|implementation|verdict>`,\n534\t  `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/jdi` (status + `run.log` + `session.log` tails),\n535\t  `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/diff` (the `mg diff` quick eyeball, `?full=1`\n536\t  for the patch), and `GET /projects/<p>/agents` (name + description only).\n537\t- **Binding + auth.** Binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default, tokenless — the\n538\t  machine's own user is the trust boundary, as it is for the CLI. A\n539\t  non-loopback bind (neither 127.0.0.0/8 nor `::1`) REQUIRES a bearer token\n540\t  (`--token`, else `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN` via the config/env layer) or the daemon\n541\t  refuses to start — the guard is unskippable, there is no flag that turns it\n542\t  off. When configured, every request must carry `Authorization: Bearer\n543\t  <token>`, compared in constant time. TLS is the reverse proxy's job\n544\t  (Caddy/nginx) — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates\n545\t  TLS, the only safe posture behind a proxy. Tokens are configured\n546\t  out-of-band; the API never issues, creates, or returns one.\n547\t- **Audit trail.** Every request is logged — timestamp, client IP, method +\n548\t  path, auth outcome (authed / tokenless / 401), response status — to the\n549\t  daemon's stderr by default. The Authorization header, the token, and request"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0a07b2b-e4d4-47fe-bb0c-9b7aa946ef87","uuid":"c5f6916e-62bb-4977-a61d-3eba84643d78","timestamp":"2026-08-28T17:48:31.231Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md","content":"control API over a registry of project roots, so any surface — a web UI, a\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client, from localhost or\nfrom a VPS. It is job one of the control-plane sequence (`mg serve` + project\nregistry + read-only API + localhost default + audit log + serialization\nskeleton); mutating endpoints, live run supervision with streamed logs, and\nany frontend are later jobs. The daemon is additive — the TUI stays\nin-process and every existing command keeps working untouched — and it is a\n**new trust boundary** with root-adjacent power (it drives docker and git and\nholds subscription credentials), so the v1 surface is deliberately read-only\nand the security invariants below are non-negotiable.\n\n- **Project registry.** An explicit config file, `<checkout>/config/serve.json`\n  (overridable via `--registry`), holding `{\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\", ...]}`.\n  No scanning, no auto-adopting directories the daemon finds. Registrations\n  are read once at startup; changing them means editing the file and\n  restarting (v1). Each entry is validated as an existing directory at\n  startup; a root without `docs/` is accepted (it lists no jobs rather than\n  failing startup). Every handler resolves ONLY against the registered roots —\n  the single choke point the zero-path-inputs rule builds on.\n- **Read-only API.** The state the TUI renders, over HTTP as JSON + raw\n  markdown (never ANSI): `GET /health` (version, docker image present,\n  per-profile readiness), `GET /projects`, `GET /projects/<p>/jobs` (the\n  TUI's info design — id/status/stage/type/date/title — plus per-job mg-jdi\n  state), `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/files/<brief|tasks|implementation|verdict>`,\n  `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/jdi` (status + `run.log` + `session.log` tails),\n  `GET /projects/<p>/jobs/<j>/diff` (the `mg diff` quick eyeball, `?full=1`\n  for the patch), and `GET /projects/<p>/agents` (name + description only).\n- **Binding + auth.** Binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default, tokenless — the\n  machine's own user is the trust boundary, as it is for the CLI. A\n  non-loopback bind (neither 127.0.0.0/8 nor `::1`) REQUIRES a bearer token\n  (`--token`, else `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN` via the config/env layer) or the daemon\n  refuses to start — the guard is unskippable, there is no flag that turns it\n  off. When configured, every request must carry `Authorization: Bearer\n  <token>`, compared in constant time. TLS is the reverse proxy's job\n  (Caddy/nginx) — the daemon always serves plain HTTP and never terminates\n  TLS, the only safe posture behind a proxy. Tokens are configured\n  out-of-band; the API never issues, creates, or returns one.\n- **Audit trail.** Every request is logged — timestamp, client IP, method +\n  path, auth outcome (authed / tokenless / 401), response status — to the\n  daemon's stderr by default. The Authorization header, the token, and request","numLines":40,"startLine":510,"totalLines":760}}}
