{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VDjSFD9MKkVhbmUHMznc1Y","type":"tool_result","content":"55\t(its branch still checked out in the main worktree itself) alike — since the\n56\tmain project root holds the user's own uncommitted work.\n57\tAgent sessions also restrict git to reading history and making commits (the\n58\tsession git shim): worktree management, branch deletes, resets, checkouts,\n59\tpushes, and the other destructive subcommands are refused.\n60\tThe manigot TUI's job detail view also offers a `t` key that opens the job's\n61\tbranch diff in tig (`mg diff <job> --tig`) in a tmux split pane / new\n62\tterminal, gated on tig being installed on the host, and an `l` key that\n63\ttails the job's `session.log` — the verbose raw stream mg-jdi writes each\n64\tinvocation's output into as it happens, distinct from the log tab's\n65\t`run.log` event summary — live in a tmux split pane / new terminal, gated on\n66\ta session.log existing for the job (created at mg-jdi run start).\n67\t`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control\n68\tAPI over a registry of project roots (an explicit config file,\n69\t`<manigot checkout>/config/serve.json`, holding `{\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\",\n70\t...]}` — no scanning; changing it means editing and restarting), so any\n71\tsurface — a web UI, a native GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client\n72\tfrom localhost or a VPS. It binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless —\n73\tthe machine's own user is the trust boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a\n74\tbearer token (`--token` or `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start,\n75\tand TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. The v1\n76\tAPI is read-only (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents,\n77\thealth) and is a new trust boundary: URL segments are never joined into\n78\tfilesystem paths, credentials are never returned in any response, every\n79\trequest is audit-logged (never the token), and mutating operations (a later\n80\tjob) must serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks` pattern.\n81\tCopying text from inside a session uses OSC 52: your terminal emulator must\n82\tsupport it, and tmux needs `set-clipboard on` when the session runs inside\n83\ttmux (mg forwards your terminal environment into the container and warns at\n84\tsession start when it detects tmux would swallow the clipboard writes — see"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0a07b2b-e4d4-47fe-bb0c-9b7aa946ef87","uuid":"3790fa89-3fbe-491a-850c-d45fd543c9a5","timestamp":"2026-08-28T17:48:31.788Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/project-template/docs/AGENTS.md","content":"(its branch still checked out in the main worktree itself) alike — since the\nmain project root holds the user's own uncommitted work.\nAgent sessions also restrict git to reading history and making commits (the\nsession git shim): worktree management, branch deletes, resets, checkouts,\npushes, and the other destructive subcommands are refused.\nThe manigot TUI's job detail view also offers a `t` key that opens the job's\nbranch diff in tig (`mg diff <job> --tig`) in a tmux split pane / new\nterminal, gated on tig being installed on the host, and an `l` key that\ntails the job's `session.log` — the verbose raw stream mg-jdi writes each\ninvocation's output into as it happens, distinct from the log tab's\n`run.log` event summary — live in a tmux split pane / new terminal, gated on\na session.log existing for the job (created at mg-jdi run start).\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a read-only control\nAPI over a registry of project roots (an explicit config file,\n`<manigot checkout>/config/serve.json`, holding `{\"projects\": [\"/abs/root\",\n...]}` — no scanning; changing it means editing and restarting), so any\nsurface — a web UI, a native GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client\nfrom localhost or a VPS. It binds `127.0.0.1:8080` by default (tokenless —\nthe machine's own user is the trust boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a\nbearer token (`--token` or `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start,\nand TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. The v1\nAPI is read-only (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents,\nhealth) and is a new trust boundary: URL segments are never joined into\nfilesystem paths, credentials are never returned in any response, every\nrequest is audit-logged (never the token), and mutating operations (a later\njob) must serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks` pattern.\nCopying text from inside a session uses OSC 52: your terminal emulator must\nsupport it, and tmux needs `set-clipboard on` when the session runs inside\ntmux (mg forwards your terminal environment into the container and warns at\nsession start when it detects tmux would swallow the clipboard writes — see","numLines":30,"startLine":55,"totalLines":126}}}
